            0.806,
            0.047
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "-000 Chapter 5 — Exploring Forces"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.083,
            0.743,
            0.122
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us now try to find out if the Earth pulls every object with equal force."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.14,
            0.482,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.9: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.18,
            0.682,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Take a spring and a few objects of different masses, such as a pencil box, a tiffin box, and a small stone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.223,
            0.683,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Hang one end of the spring from a nail (Fig. 5.12a). From the other end, hang an object and observe the spring (Fig. 5.12b). Does the spring stretch?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.283,
            0.683,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Now hang the other objects, one by one and notice the stretch in the spring each time. Is the stretch caused by each object the same?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.344,
            0.684,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When an object is hung from a spring, the spring stretches due to the force applied on the object by the Earth. We find that the stretch caused in the spring is different for different objects. This indicates that the Earth pulls different objects with different forces, that is, the weight of different objects is different. Can we use the spring to measure the weight of an object?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.695,
            0.132,
            0.932,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.695,
                0.132,
                0.932,
                0.429
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.697,
                0.431,
                0.942,
                0.48
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.12: (a) A hanging spring; (b) Two different objects hung from the spring"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.487,
            0.311,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A step further"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.523,
            0.737,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A spring balance is a simple device used to measure weight (force). It consists of a spring fixed at one end, with a hook attached at the other end. When we hang an object from the hook, the spring stretches, and the amount of stretching gives the weight of the object. There is a scale on the balance which is marked to show the weight (force) in newton. Usually, there is also another scale to show the corresponding values of mass in gram (g). These values have been marked with the assumption that the spring balance is used on the Earth, with the Earth’s gravitational force attracting the object."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.551,
            0.917,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.741,
                0.551,
                0.917,
                0.684
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.695,
            0.744,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us learn to measure the weight using a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "spring balance",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". But first, let us familiarise ourselves with a spring balance the way we did with thermometer earlier (in the chapter ‘Temperature and Its Measurement’ in Curiosity, Grade 6)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.788,
            0.503,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.10: Let us observe"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.828,
            0.743,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Look at the spring balance shown in Fig. 5.13 carefully. What is the maximum weight it can measure?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.871,
            0.743,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The maximum weight it can measure is 10 N. Thus, this scale has a range of 0 to 10 N."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 16,
